  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nTropical Storm Edouard continues to move toward the Upper Texas \nCoast and is forecast to make landfall late Tuesday/early Wednesday. \nWhat does that translate for us here in South Texas? Unfortunately \nfor most, just some extra high clouds. For those closer to the \nVictoria Crossroads, they'll have increased chances for scattered \nshowers/thunderstorms (20-30% chances) this afternoon through \nWednesday morning. Most of the highest totals will remain generally \nalong and east of the I-45 corridor, and only a few tenths of an \ninch near the Victoria area. We'll continue to monitor the low track \nas any wobble could give the Victoria Crossroads closer to the \nhigher PWATs (>2.10\") meaning more efficient rainfall rates. Given \nthe distance from Edouard, a moderate risk for rip currents will \nexist north of Port Aransas through Wednesday. While the extra cloud \ncoverage will be welcomed, the HeatRisk will continue to top out in \nthe major/extreme risk level (level 3 and 4 of 4) most afternoons. \n\nMid-level high pressure across the Southeast US will continue to \nkeep the hot weather in play through the weekend, but it will also \nallow weak disturbances to move to the west around the southern \nperiphery in the Gulf. What this mean for this Labor Day weekend \nwill be increased chances for seabreeze showers/storms with greatest \nchances for storms primarily confined to the coastal plains. While \nnot a complete washout, anyone planning outdoor activities with few \nlocations for shelters (like the beach), will need to keep the car \nunlocked for quick access in the event of a shower (and the \noccasional AC break).",
